{ While slightly weaker theoretically compared to 3...c4, practically Nf6, the Closed Variation of the French Tarrasch, gives black stronger chances at a win. This response puts more pressure on the e4 pawn, pressuring it to move, trade, or be defended. }
4. e5!! { White's strongest, most common response. This advances the pawn, removing it from pressure from black's pawn and knight, while also pressuring black's knight, forcing it to move again, gaining back any developmental tempo loss. This comes at the cost of no longer pressuring black's d5 pawn and locking the central position down to a closed structure, which black argues is advantageous for black. } (4. Bd3 { White's 2nd most common response. This develops the light-squared bishop, defending the e4 pawn again while bringing white closer to castling king's side. This comes at the cost of obstructing the queen's defense of the d4 pawn even further. }) (4. exd5? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This trades off the pressured e4 pawn, reducing tension in the center. However, this essentially wastes the reason for white developing its knight with Nd2, to defend the e4 pawn without having to play exd5, now leaving it as simply an obstruction of the queen and dark-squared bishop. }) (4. Ngf3?? { Uncommon response. Blunder. This attempts to develop white's king's side knight. However, this doesn't provide any defense of e4, and allows black to simply capture and force white to lose tempo moving its knight. } 4... dxe4 { Lost pawn and tempo. }) 4... Nfd7!! { Black's strongest, most common response. This removes the knight from danger, pressures the e5 pawn, obstructs any checks on black's king, and defends the c5 square for future c5 pawn advances. } (4... Ne4? { Black's 2nd most common response. Mistake. This attempts to aggressively centralize the pressured knight, offering a knight trade. However, white gains an advantage with this trade, as this doubles black's pawns and leaves the forward pawn overextended and easy to capture. } 5. Nxe4 dxe4 { Overextended e4 pawn. }) (4... Ng8? { Black's 3rd most common response. Mistake. This attempts to retreat the knight, in order to reroute the knight with Ne7 or Nh6 later. However, this results in a strict loss of tempo and limits the movement for the knight more than Nfd7, allowing white to gain a developmental advantage. }) *


