[UTCTime "19:34:21"]

{ Black removes its knight from danger while it pressures the e5 pawn, obstructs any checks on black's king, and defends the c5 square for future c5 pawn advances. }
5. Bd3 { White's most common response. This develops the light-squared bishop, bringing white closer to castling king's side, while also preparing a potential discovery on d4 after the d2 knight moves. This also avoids committing the king's side knight to either e2 or f3, allowing flexibility. } (5. f4! { Pawn Center Variation. White's 2nd most common, strong response, seen more at low-level, likely due to its practically difficult play even at high-level, resulting in more wins and draws for black than other responses. This begins a king's side pawn attack while adding another defender to the e5 pawn, creating a strong pawn center. This also allows white to play Ngf3 without obstructing the f-pawn. Transposes with c3 with best play. }) (5. c3! { White's 3rd most common, strong response. This defends the d4 pawn and creates a large pawn chain. This response is stronger considering white's queen's side knight has already moved with Nd2, so the pawn isn't potentially obstructing the knight's development. Transposes with f4, the Center Pawn Variation, with best play. }) (5. Ngf3?! { White's 4th most common response. Inaccuracy. This develops the king's side knight, defending the two forward pawns while bringing white closer to castling king's side. However, this obstructs any movement of the f2 pawn, and later on this actually somewhat obstructs the movement of the d2 knight, which can want to move to f3 itself. Because of this, the king's side knight is actually better off being moved to e2 later on, which ends up being a slightly stronger method of overall development. }) (5. Ndf3? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This repositions the knight to f3, defending the forward pawns. However, moving the knight twice early in the game leaves white with a developmental disadvantage that black can take advantage of by immediately breaking open the center with c5. }) (5. c4?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This attempts to attack black's pawn chain. However, black can counterattack with c5, opening up the position favorably for black since white's e5 pawn is slightly overextended. }) *


