{ White develops its light-squared bishop, bringing white closer to castling king's side, while also preparing a potential discovery on d4 after the d2 knight moves. }
5... c5!! { Black's strongest, most common response. This immediately attacks white's pawn center, threatening to take with cxd4 if white doesn't trade pawns or reinforce the pawn. } (5... Be7?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This attempts to develop the dark-squared bishop so that black can castle king's side. However, this allows white to aggressively develop its queen with Qg4, forcing black to develop awkwardly to prevent various mate threats, which can be used by white to win pieces or go up an exchange. }) (5... b6? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This attempts to provide additional support to c5, while also allowing the light-squared bishop to fianchetto. However, this doesn't develop a minor piece or immediately threaten the center, giving white tempo to gain an even greater lead in development. Often transposes to the Botvinnik Variation, which will not be covered in this study. }) (5... a6?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This attempts to defend b5. However, this doesn't develop a minor piece or immediately threaten the center, giving white tempo to gain an even greater lead in development. }) *


