{ Black immediately attacks white's pawn center with c5, threatening to take with cxd4 if white doesn't trade pawns or reinforce the pawn. }
6. c3! { White's strongest, most common response. This simply reinforces the d4 pawn forming a large pawn chain, which was the whole point of playing the Tarrasch Variation. } (6. Ne2?? { Uncommon response. Blunder. This attempts to defend the d4 pawn. However, this neglects that white's d4 pawn is defending the e5 pawn, which can be captured with Nxe5 after a pawn trade with cxd4. }) (6. Ngf3? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This develops the knight, attempting to defend the d4 pawn. However, this causes the knight to be overloaded defending the overextended e5 pawn, which lets black simply take with cxd4. White can usually recapture the pawn, but its attacking chances are severely crippled. }) (6. c4?? { Uncommon response. Blunder. This attempts to counterattack black's pawn chain. However, this neglects that white's d4 pawn is defending the e5 pawn, which can be captured with Nxe5 after a pawn trade with cxd4. }) (6. dxc5?? { Uncommon response. Blunder. This attempts to trade pawns to give white time to defend one of the pawns. However, after Nxe5, white has doubled pawns, is poised to lose the bishop pair with Nxd3, and its c5 pawn is overextended, likely to be taken. }) *


