{ White simply reinforces its d4 pawn with c3, forming a large pawn chain, which was the whole point of playing the Tarrasch Variation. }
6... Nc6 { Black's most common, strong response. This puts more pressure on the d4 and e5 pawns, threatening a pawn trade and Nxd4. Transposes with cxd4 with best play. } (6... cxd4 { Black's 2nd most common, strong response. This simply trades pawns, reducing some tension in the center in preparation to pressure d4 with Nc6. Transposes with Nc6 with best play. }) (6... b6!? { Botvinnik Variation. Black's 3rd most common response, seen more at high-level. Mistake. This attempts to provide additional support to c5, while also allowing the light-squared bishop to fianchetto. However, this doesn't develop a minor piece or immediately threaten the center, giving white tempo to gain an even greater lead in development. Despite this, b6 sees good practical results at high-level, likely due to rarity. This variation will not be covered in this study. }) (6... Qb6?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This aggressively develops the queen, pressuring the d4 and b2 pawns. However, this doesn't actually threaten anything yet as white can easily defend the pawn with Ne2 and this leaves the black queen slightly out of position. }) (6... c4?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This attacks white's light-squared bishop while closing off the center, giving black a large pawn chain. However, white gains an advantage with a closed center, allowing it to safely continue development to begin a king's side attack. }) (6... Be7?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This attempts to develop the dark-squared bishop so that black can castle king's side. However, this allows white to aggressively develop its queen with Qg4, forcing black to develop awkwardly to prevent various mate threats, which can be used by white to win pieces or go up an exchange. }) *


