{ Black puts more pressure on the d4 and e5 pawns with Nc6, threatening a pawn trade and Nxd4. }
7. Ne2 { White's most common, strong response. This develops the king's side knight, defending the d4 pawn while also not obstructing the f3 square, which allows white to continue to support the d4 square with Nf3 if necessary. } (7. Ngf3?! { White's 2nd most common response. Inaccuracy. This develops the king's side knight, attempting to defend the d4 square. However, this obstructs the d2 knight from being able to move to defend the d4 square, as Nb3 leads to a pawn fork of the knight and light-squared bishop. This allows black to open the position slightly, which is slightly advantageous for black. }) (7. Ndf3 { White's 3rd most common, strong response. This defends the d4 pawn, while allowing white to continue to support the d4 pawn with Ne2 if necessary. }) (7. Qg4? { Uncommon response. Mistake. This attempts to aggressively develop the queen, defending the d4 square and pressuring the g7 pawn, preventing black from moving its dark-squared bishop and therefore castling king's side. However, the queen can be pressured away from defense of d4 with h5 and then g5, which leads into a king's side pawn attack, which is stronger since black hasn't castled kings' side. }) *


