[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppn1ppp/4p3/3pP3/3P1P2/8/PPPN2PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2023.05.23"]
[UTCTime "14:13:01"]

{ White begins a king's side pawn attack while adding another defender to the e5 pawn with f4, creating a strong pawn center. This also allows white to play Ngf3 without obstructing the f-pawn. Black has one main way to respond to white. }
5... c5!! { Black's strongest, universal response. This attacks white's pawn center by offering a pawn trade, threatening to win a central pawn from white in exchange for a flank pawn. } (5... Be7?! { Rare response. Inaccuracy. This develops the dark-squared bishop in order to quickly castle king's side. However, this gives white critical tempo to consolidate its large center with c3. } 6. c3 c5 7. Ndf3 Nc6 8. Bd3 cxd4 9. cxd4 { White has consolidated its center, disallowing black from developing easily. }) 6. c3!! { (0.65@52) White's strongest, most common response. This supports the d4 pawn, avoiding the loss of a center pawn after a trade. Being able to play c3 is the entire reason for playing the Tarrasch with Nd2 over the mainline Nc3. } (6. Ngf3 { White's 2nd most common response. This develops the king's side knight, defending the d4 and e5 pawns while also preventing any Qh4+ checks. This also brings white closer to castling king's side. Note that Nf3 no longer obstructs the f-pawn since it has been advanced forward already. }) (6. Ndf3 { White's 3rd most common response. This defends the d4 and e5 pawns, prevents any Qh4+ checks, and opens up the queen's defense of the d4 pawn. This appears to obstruct white's king's side knight from developing to f3, however, this allows white to defend the d4 pawn twice after playing Ne2 (after developing the light-squared bishop), a tactic seen in several mainline Tarrasch defenses. }) *


