{ Black attacks white's pawn center by offering a pawn trade with c5, threatening to win a central pawn from white in exchange for a flank pawn. In response, white defends the d4 pawn with c3, preventing the loss of a central pawn after any exchange. Black has a few options to continue building pressure on the d4 pawn. }
6... Nc6! { (0.54@54) Black's most common, strong response. This develops the queen's side knight, putting additional pressure on both of white's d4 and e5 pawns. Transposes with Qb6 with best play. } (6... Qb6! { Black's 2nd most common, strong response. This develops the queen to a very active square, pressuring both the b2 pawn and the d4 pawn with an x-ray attack. This pressure on the b2 pawn discourages white from later developing its dark-squared bishop, but there are many ways to gambit the b2 pawn for advantage. Qb6 also puts x-ray pressure on the f2 and g1 squares, allowing potential later tactical attacks on white's king before or after castling king's side. Transposes with Nc6 with best play. } 7. Ndf3!! { White's strongest, most common response. } (7. Ngf3 { White's 2nd most common response. }) (7. Nb3 { Uncommon response. })) (6... cxd4 { Black's 3rd most common response. This relieves pressure in the center by trading pawns, opening up the queen's side somewhat. } 7. cxd4!! { White's strongest, universal response. This simply recaptures the pawn, maintaining white's center. }) 7. Ndf3!! { (0.54@50) White's strongest, most common response. } (7. Ngf3 { (0.54@50) White's 2nd most common response. }) (7. Nb3 { (0.45@50) Uncommon response. }) *


